***************************************************************************
                                 Sigils
***************************************************************************

Sigils are passive, levelled effects supplied by active equipment or by an
explicit inherent source on a living. They modify existing game actions; a
sigil should not grant a command of its own.

This file covers the builder-facing contracts. Players can use "sigils" to
see their active recognized sigils and "sigils catalog" to see the effects
their character recognizes. See wizhelp 4.26 for equipment power and
gearcheck generally.

Applicant-level and higher wizards also have two diagnostic forms:

====================
sigils <player>
sigils report
====================

"sigils <player>" shows the same source breakdown for another online living
or a saved player. "sigils report" summarizes every currently online
non-immortal player, including players with no active recognized sigils. The
report excludes NPCs and Applicant-level or higher users. Both forms apply
the viewing wizard's normal hidden-sigil discovery rules.

The system has four main owners:

    /secure/include/sigils.h
        Stable IDs and display/scoring metadata.

    /inherits/equip.c
        The API used to place sigils on equipment.

    /secure/living/equipment.c
        Active equipment and inherent-source aggregation.

    /secure/living/sigils.c
        Shared effect calculations, cooldowns, counters, discovery, and
        effect messages.

Do not put a standard sigil's mechanical implementation on every marked
item. The item contributes levels; the shared living layer implements the
effect once.

***************************************************************************
                        Putting Sigils on Equipment
***************************************************************************

Weapons, armour, implants, and other objects inheriting EQUIP already have
the sigil API. Configure a mark with its lowercase registry ID:

====================
set_sigil("rime focus", 1);
====================

An item can carry more than one sigil:

====================
set_sigil("fortified", 1);
set_sigil("venom ward", 1);
====================

|CKBvoid set_sigil(string sSigil, int iLevel);|XX0
        Adds or replaces the item's contribution for a registered sigil.
        The ID is normalized to lowercase. Unknown IDs and levels below 1
        remove that entry. Values above the registry maximum are clamped.

|CKBvoid remove_sigil(string sSigil);|XX0
        Removes the named contribution from the item.

|CKBint query_sigil(string sSigil);|XX0
        Returns this item's contribution, or 0 when absent.

|CKBmapping query_sigils();|XX0
        Returns the item's valid sigil contributions, clamped to their
        registry maxima. Use this public query in consumers.

The sigil is active only while the item participates in the living's active
equipment set. That includes worn armour, main and secondary weapons, and
properly activated internal equipment. Merely carrying an item is not enough.
Use the normal armour, weapon, or internal-equipment lifecycle; do not make a
carried object alter the player's inherent sigil mapping as a shortcut.

Contributions from active sources add together and the final level is capped.
Excess levels have no effect. Ordinary marked equipment usually contributes
one level. A two-level contribution is a deliberate reward and balance choice,
not a default. Review the item's slot, acquisition difficulty, item level, and
the ease of completing the sigil's cap.

Looking at an item automatically shows recognized sigils and their item/max
levels. The "sigils" command automatically reports active totals and their
equipment sources. Builders do not need to duplicate either display.

An explicit successful "get" or "take" calls the living's
notify_sigil_pickup() helper. query_sigil_pickup_message() owns the generic
text used by that notification. Neither helper names sigils, changes discovery
state, or runs during unrelated inventory delivery and restoration paths.

Sigils are scored automatically by EQUIPMENT_POWER_D at:

====================
item contribution * registry power per level
====================

Do not add add_power_feature() or set_power_adjustment() for the same sigil.
That would count its power twice. Use "gearcheck <item>" to inspect the
result; hidden sigils are still scored even when their identity is concealed.

***************************************************************************
                         Inherent Sigil Sources
***************************************************************************

Livings have a separate inherent sigil mapping for forms, boons, guild
features, and other non-equipment sources:

====================
player->set_sigil("evade", 1);
player->remove_sigil("evade");
player->query_inherent_sigil("evade");
====================

This setter replaces the entire inherent value for that ID. It does not keep
independent contributions for several systems. If several features can grant
the same inherent sigil, one owning soul, daemon, or inheritable must retain
their primitive source state, calculate the combined value, and publish that
value through set_sigil(). Do not have unrelated objects race to overwrite or
remove each other's contribution.

Temporary inherent sources must reapply through their normal restore or
activation path and remove their contribution on every exit path, including
expiry, form change, guild cleanup, destruction, quit, and reconnect where
applicable. Prefer equipment sigils when a concrete equipped object is the
actual source; equipment aggregation already owns that lifecycle.

Useful living queries are:

|CKBint query_sigil(string sSigil);|XX0
        Returns the capped effective total from inherent and active-equipment
        sources. Mechanics should use this query.

|CKBmapping query_sigils();|XX0
        Returns all capped effective totals.

|CKBint query_equipment_sigil(string sSigil);|XX0
        Returns the uncapped sum from active equipment only.

|CKBmapping query_equipment_sigils();|XX0
        Returns all uncapped active-equipment sums.

|CKBint query_inherent_sigil(string sSigil);|XX0
        Returns the living's inherent contribution only.

The uncapped source queries are useful for presentation and diagnostics. Game
effects should consume query_sigil() so excess sources cannot exceed the
mechanical maximum.

***************************************************************************
                         Extending the Registry
***************************************************************************

Sigil IDs are player-visible compatibility contracts. Before adding one,
search all producers and consumers, confirm that an existing sigil cannot
express the design, and settle the effect, cap, progression band, power per
level, acquisition plan, and exact integration boundary.

Add the definition to SIGILS in /secure/include/sigils.h:

====================
"open mark": SIGIL_DEF(3, 100, "mid",
  "Its concise player-facing effect summary"),
====================

Each definition supplies:

    max
        Maximum effective level. Current effects support a maximum of 3,
        except Critical Strike and Ruthless, which support 5. A new maximum
        is not metadata-only: update and test query_sigil() in
        /secure/living/equipment.c and query_sigil_effect_level() in
        /secure/living/sigils.c rather than allowing display and mechanics
        to disagree.

    power
        Equipment power per contributed level. Set this from expected value
        in ordinary play, not the largest possible activation. It is scored
        automatically as utility by the current power daemon.

    band
        The player-facing progression label: "low", "mid", or "high".
        This is catalog metadata, not an equip restriction.

    summary
        Concise player-facing text used by "sigils catalog". Describe what
        the sigil does, without implementation jargon.

    hidden
        Supplied by SIGIL_DEF() or HIDDEN_SIGIL_DEF(). See Hidden Sigils
        below.

Keep the ID lowercase and stable. Renaming an ID changes equipment source,
effect, discovery, display, tests, and possibly saved character progression.
Preserve an alias or migrate persisted discovery state if a live hidden ID
must change.

***************************************************************************
                         Implementing the Effect
***************************************************************************

Implement shared behavior in /secure/living/sigils.c. Read levels through
query_sigil() or query_sigil_effect_level(); do not rescan worn inventory or
reimplement source aggregation. Keep the operation bounded, validate object
arguments, and avoid retaining LPC object references in persistent state.

Use the narrowest existing integration boundary. Current examples include:

    /secure/living.c
        Damage dealt/received, fatal damage, kills, shields, and regen.

    /secure/living/combat.c
        Critical-hit and dodge calculations.

    /secure/living/stats.c
        Overhealing.

    /inherits/ash_burner.c
        Ash yield and autosalvage proceeds.

    /objects/professions/profession_material.c
        Profession-material quantity.

Prefer a named helper in the sigil effect layer with one call at the owning
gameplay boundary. Do not scatter string-ID checks through item files or
several unrelated callers. Add a compatible forward declaration in the
consumer when that file's compilation style requires one.

Runtime cooldowns, counters, object references, and caches must be nosave.
Use stable string keys for independent counters and cooldowns. Messages about
a hidden effect must pass through the shared hidden-aware presentation helper
or provide equivalent concealment; a hidden ID must not leak through combat
spam before discovery.

When adding a reactive effect, decide and test:

* whether it triggers before or after ordinary mitigation;
* whether misses, zero damage, reflected damage, and self-damage count;
* whether repeated hits retain state across targets or combat;
* whether cooldowns are per living, per target, or global to that effect;
* whether NPCs and players use the same behavior;
* whether the effect can recurse through the event it creates;
* what happens on death, linkdeath, reconnect, and destruction.

Use catch() only at optional integration boundaries where a sigil failure must
not break the owning action. The effect itself should remain deterministic
where practical and must not mutate state from a display or power query.

***************************************************************************
                            Hidden Sigils
***************************************************************************

Use a hidden definition only when discovering the mark is real progression:

====================
"secret mark": HIDDEN_SIGIL_DEF(3, 160, "high",
  "Its concise player-facing effect summary"),
====================

Hidden sigils remain mechanically active and fully power-scored. Until a
character discovers one, item inspection reports an uninterpretable marking,
gearcheck reports an undiscovered sigil, the active list and catalog omit its
identity, and generic effect messages must not reveal its name.

The system that owns the actual discovery criterion reveals it only after the
criterion succeeds:

====================
if (player->discover_sigil("secret mark"))
  /* criterion-specific follow-up, if any */
====================

discover_sigil() returns true only for a newly learned hidden ID, persists the
player immediately, and normally sends a generic discovery message. Pass a
nonzero second argument when the owning quest, encounter, remort, or lore
interaction supplies its own message:

====================
player->discover_sigil("secret mark", 1);
====================

query_sigil_known() returns true for public or discovered registry IDs.
query_discovered_sigils() returns a defensive copy of learned hidden IDs.
Do not use item possession, display text, or an object's alias as discovery
state. Do not add hidden definitions to public exhaustive wiki tables.

***************************************************************************
                              Validation
***************************************************************************

For ordinary marked gear:

* compile the item with both standard LPC checks;
* look at it before and after equipping it;
* verify "sigils" reports the correct source and capped total;
* verify removing, unwielding, or extracting it removes the contribution;
* inspect "gearcheck <item>" and confirm the sigil is scored once;
* exercise save/login and reconnect when the equipment kind requires it.

For a new or changed sigil, extend the focused regression in
/secure/obj/sigil_effects_test.c. Validate the registry entry, level 1, the
maximum level, one level beyond the cap, and the exact mechanical boundary.
Update the regression's expected registry count when adding or retiring an
entry. For chance effects, bound the result rather than asserting one random
roll. For counters and cooldowns, test one event before activation,
activation, and the immediate repeat or reset path.

Compile every changed LPC file and representative consumers of a changed
shared include or living inheritable. Then exercise the effect through its
real gameplay action on a live or test MUD. Compilation alone does not prove
equipment activation, cleanup, discovery persistence, or event ordering.

